WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.048
Hello, everyone! I'm Mr. Daniel. Welcome to Online Upper-Intermediate.

2
00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:08.340
Hi! I'm Nita. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:15.576
Unit ten: Media and Truth. In this unit, you will discuss journalism, truth and report what people claimed.

4
00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:17.573
press freedom.

5
00:00:18.273 --> 00:00:22.513
press freedom means the right of journalists to report without government control.

6
00:00:22.813 --> 00:00:25.033
Press freedom is essential in a democracy.

7
00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:28.269
Your turn. Say: press freedom.

8
00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:32.238
press freedom.

9
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.924
investigative journalism.

10
00:00:35.624 --> 00:00:40.225
investigative journalism means reporting that carefully researches hidden problems.

11
00:00:40.525 --> 00:00:43.349
Investigative journalism exposed the scandal.

12
00:00:44.049 --> 00:00:47.328
Your turn. Say: investigative journalism.

13
00:00:50.028 --> 00:00:51.853
investigative journalism.

14
00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:53.932
whistleblower.

15
00:00:54.632 --> 00:00:59.093
whistleblower means a person who reveals wrongdoing inside an organisation.

16
00:00:59.393 --> 00:01:01.822
The whistleblower revealed the company's lies.

17
00:01:02.522 --> 00:01:04.873
Your turn. Say: whistleblower.

18
00:01:07.573 --> 00:01:08.806
whistleblower.

19
00:01:09.817 --> 00:01:10.644
tabloid.

20
00:01:11.344 --> 00:01:16.106
tabloid means a newspaper with short, sensational stories about celebrities.

21
00:01:16.406 --> 00:01:18.986
Tabloids love stories about famous couples.

22
00:01:19.686 --> 00:01:21.747
Your turn. Say: tabloid.

23
00:01:24.447 --> 00:01:25.285
tabloid.

24
00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:27.447
sensationalism.

25
00:01:28.147 --> 00:01:32.329
sensationalism means presenting news in a shocking way to attract attention.

26
00:01:32.629 --> 00:01:36.184
Sensationalism makes small problems look like disasters.

27
00:01:36.884 --> 00:01:39.258
Your turn. Say: sensationalism.

28
00:01:41.958 --> 00:01:43.400
sensationalism.

29
00:01:44.417 --> 00:01:45.522
objectivity.

30
00:01:46.222 --> 00:01:49.754
objectivity means reporting facts without personal opinions.

31
00:01:50.054 --> 00:01:52.077
Good journalists aim for objectivity.

32
00:01:52.777 --> 00:01:55.081
Your turn. Say: objectivity.

33
00:01:57.781 --> 00:01:58.898
objectivity.

34
00:01:59.883 --> 00:02:00.757
fact-checker.

35
00:02:01.457 --> 00:02:05.209
fact-checker means a person who checks whether information is true.

36
00:02:05.509 --> 00:02:07.938
The fact-checker found three errors in the article.

37
00:02:08.638 --> 00:02:10.803
Your turn. Say: fact-checker.

38
00:02:13.503 --> 00:02:14.678
fact-checker.

39
00:02:15.683 --> 00:02:16.452
deepfake.

40
00:02:17.152 --> 00:02:20.638
deepfake means a fake video made with AI that looks real.

41
00:02:20.938 --> 00:02:23.715
The deepfake showed the president saying things he never said.

42
00:02:24.415 --> 00:02:26.638
Your turn. Say: deepfake.

43
00:02:29.338 --> 00:02:30.351
deepfake.

44
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.408
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:33.708 --> 00:02:37.196
Did you see the video of the minister? Everyone's sharing it.

46
00:02:38.483 --> 00:02:41.017
A fact-checker warned that it might be a deepfake.

47
00:02:42.317 --> 00:02:45.143
Really? The tabloid claimed it was real.

48
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:51.285
Tabloids often rely on sensationalism. Did any serious newspaper confirm it?

49
00:02:52.583 --> 00:02:56.304
No. One journalist admitted that she hadn't checked the source.

50
00:02:57.617 --> 00:03:01.311
That's why investigative journalism and press freedom matter so much.

51
00:03:02.617 --> 00:03:05.290
Grammar focus: Reporting verbs.

52
00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.007
Reporting verbs summarise what people said.

53
00:03:08.307 --> 00:03:14.747
Claim and refuse take to plus verb. Admit and deny take the -ing form. Warn and advise take an object plus to.

54
00:03:15.047 --> 00:03:19.601
For example: She denied sharing the video, and they warned us not to trust the post.

55
00:03:19.901 --> 00:03:24.605
Reporting verbs summarise what someone said, so you don't need to report every word.

56
00:03:25.617 --> 00:03:28.757
Now read and listen. Can We Still Trust What We See?.

57
00:03:29.057 --> 00:03:33.041
Deepfake technology can now create videos that look completely real.

58
00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:38.021
Experts have warned that fake videos could influence elections.

59
00:03:39.317 --> 00:03:43.673
Some politicians have denied making statements that appeared in viral clips.

60
00:03:44.983 --> 00:03:49.467
Fact-checkers suggest checking at least two reliable sources before sharing.

61
00:03:50.783 --> 00:03:55.024
In this new world, objectivity and press freedom are more important than ever.

62
00:03:56.317 --> 00:03:58.667
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

63
00:03:58.967 --> 00:04:02.430
A person who reveals wrongdoing in an organisation is a blank.

64
00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:06.186
A, whistleblower. B, tabloid. C, fact-checker.

65
00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:12.206
The answer is A: whistleblower.

66
00:04:13.717 --> 00:04:15.043
He denied blank the money.

67
00:04:15.343 --> 00:04:18.485
A, to take. B, taking. C, take.

68
00:04:22.483 --> 00:04:24.111
The answer is B: taking.

69
00:04:25.617 --> 00:04:27.732
She promised blank the report by Friday.

70
00:04:28.032 --> 00:04:31.407
A, finishing. B, to finish. C, finish.

71
00:04:35.417 --> 00:04:37.196
The answer is B: to finish.

72
00:04:38.683 --> 00:04:41.136
A fake video made with AI is a blank.

73
00:04:41.436 --> 00:04:45.275
A, deepfake. B, sensationalism. C, objectivity.

74
00:04:49.283 --> 00:04:51.202
The answer is A: deepfake.

75
00:04:52.717 --> 00:04:56.376
According to the text, what do fact-checkers suggest?

76
00:04:56.676 --> 00:05:02.501
A, sharing quickly. B, checking two reliable sources. C, trusting viral clips.

77
00:05:06.517 --> 00:05:09.875
The answer is B: checking two reliable sources.

78
00:05:11.383 --> 00:05:15.115
Now it's your turn to speak. How do you decide if a news story is true?

79
00:05:20.415 --> 00:05:26.873
I check who published it, look for the same story in reliable sources, and I'm careful with sensational headlines.

80
00:05:27.173 --> 00:05:28.581
Excellent answer, Nita!

81
00:05:29.583 --> 00:05:33.466
Great work today! You learned eight new words and reporting verbs.

82
00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:36.697
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

83
00:05:36.997 --> 00:05:39.475
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

84
00:05:39.775 --> 00:05:42.892
Thank you, Mr. Daniel! Bye, everyone!

